i'm going to talk a little bit about how  we made the first picture of a black  hole  scientists have been studying black  holes ever since they were first  predicted  from einstein's theory of general  relativity just over 100 years ago  and in particular for decades scientists  have been studying the giant elliptical  galaxy  at the head of the virgo constellation  so this galaxy which is called m87  is really special it's 55 million light  years away from us  and over 100 years ago someone  discovered this streak of light on the  sky  and it ended up being this galactic  scale jet of plasma that was shooting  out of the core of the galaxy  and marking the spot of a supermassive  black hole so over  decades scientists tried to develop  better and better instruments  telescopes to try to study the black  hole predicted to be at its center  and then in april of 2017 we hooked up  this earth's ice telescope  and collected the data necessary to make  the first image of a black hole  and two years later after processing the  data this is what we saw  and this black hole image was the result  of a group of hundreds of astronomers  physicists  mathematicians engineers from all around  the world  who are working together to achieve  something that wouldn't have been  possible for any one person to do  so the first question you might ask is  how are we able to take a picture of a  black hole that by definition doesn't  let light escape  but light propagating near a black hole  actually doesn't follow straight lines  space and time are actually curved by  the black hole's gravity  and this means the light can even go in  complete circles around it  so some of this light is falls into the  black hole but other ones just  graze it it's bent around it so that the  net effect is essentially that the black  hole casts the shadow  on a backdrop of hot material that's  almost perfectly circular  um and this ring is referred to as a  black hole shadow because it's like the  imprint of the black hole on all this  hot gas behind it  this is what astrophysicists think a  black hole would actually look like up  close  this ring is really really tiny it's  about 40 microarch seconds in size  which is about the same size as a grain  of sand but when that sand  is in boston and i'm viewing it from  here in california  so it's like you held up a grain of sand  at mit and  i had to try to see it from here in  california and so taking a picture  of something that small is really hard  the reason why it can all be explained  with a very simple equation  diffraction says that there are  fundamental limits to the smallest  objects we can see  just like our eyes can't see like  bacteria and different things like this  uh telescopes also have fundamental  limits of what they can see  and this governing equation says that in  order to see smaller and smaller  we have to make our telescope bigger and  bigger so if we plug in the size of the  black hole we want to see and the  wavelength we have to see it at we can  easily calculate that we just need to  build a telescope  13 million meters across or essentially  the size of the earth  and if we could build this earth-sized  telescope we could just start to make  out that ring of light that's indicative  of the black hole's event horizon  well okay obviously building a single  dish telescope the size of the earth  isn't possible but by joining telescopes  located around the world  i've been working as part of an  international collaboration  called the event horizon telescope that  built a computational telescope  the size of the earth that was capable  of resolving structure on the scale of a  black hole event for heisen  for the very first time so how does this  computational telescope work  well a radio telescope is they act  essentially just like giant mirrors  light from the black hole it's going to  travel the earth for 55 million years  and then it's going to bounce off that  dish and collect at the focal point here  and all the light is going to collect at  that one location  but now let's imagine that instead of  building a single  earth-sized dish instead we laid mirrors  all across the earth  essentially turning the earth into a  giant spinning disco ball  well then we could collect the light at  each one of these tiny little mirrors  record the light that we see at each  location and then we could combine the  individually collected light in a  computer  to make a picture just as good as the  original one that we could get with a  single  mirror but what happens if we remove  most of these mirrors so only a few  remain  well that's exactly what the event  horizon telescope has to deal with  so all of the researchers in the  worldwide network work together  so so once we have gotten these tiny  little signals  then this question remains of how do we  make a picture right  how do we form a picture from all this  data because we only have telescopes at  a few locations  that means we only get a small number of  measurements and so  what are these measurements you can  think of them each one as being notes in  a song  so watching the black hole with the  event horizon telescope is a bit like  listening to a song being played on a  piano that has a lot of broken keys  if you were able to recognize the song  what you're doing is your brain is  filling in the holes to kind of tell you  what you're listening to  so this is really similar to how we  dealt with making an image of the black  hole  we designed algorithms that would use  the sparse data we collect and they  would fill in the missing notes to try  to reconstruct the underlying black hole  image  and then after seven weeks we all  gathered together in cambridge  massachusetts  and we revealed the images to each other  and this is what we saw  a ring of roughly the same size that was  brighter on the bottom than the top  this image tells us so much about the  immediate environment around a black  hole  but perhaps one of the most amazing  things is that by comparing this picture  to simulation scientists have made for  years  stimulations that predict what the  emission around the black hole would  look like assuming einstein was right  about gravity  we find that the image we took was  amazingly consistent with the number of  these predictions and so  by studying this image we have the best  evidence as of yet that black holes  exist  as well as a way to study and learn  about the immediate environment around a  black hole  how the matter falls onto them and how  they impact their galaxies around them  so i hope going forward you have the  chance to work with some amazing people  go in  unexpected directions and do something  that you also thought wasn't possible  you
 

